{"findings":[{"criterionId":"claim","observation":"You described the two plates, but your paragraph doesn't say which plate melted the ice faster. Start with a sentence that answers the question directly, such as \"The ___ plate melts ice faster than the ___ plate.\"","suggestedLevel":"beginning","excerpts":[{"artifact":"work","quote":"we had two plates, one shiny and one blue"}],"uncertainty":"low"},{"criterionId":"evidence","observation":"You mention timing the melting, but you don't give any times or describe the three trials for each plate. Add the actual melting times for both plates so a reader can compare them. Saying \"it took a long time\" doesn't show which plate was faster.","suggestedLevel":"beginning","excerpts":[{"artifact":"work","quote":"my partner timed it on his phone"},{"artifact":"work","quote":"It took a long time"}],"uncertainty":"low"},{"criterionId":"reasoning","observation":"You correctly noticed that the ice melted because the room was warm, and you identified melting as a change of state. To explain the difference between the plates, describe how thermal energy moves from the warmer plate into the ice cube, and why one plate transfers energy to the ice faster than the other.","suggestedLevel":"beginning","excerpts":[{"artifact":"work","quote":"The room was warm so thats why the ice melted."},{"artifact":"work","quote":"Ice is solid and when it melts it turns into liquid water, that is a change of state."}],"uncertainty":"medium"}]}
```

```json
{"findings":[{"criterionId":"claim","observation":"Your claim directly answers the question by stating which plate melts ice faster. It is clear and easy to find at the start of your paragraph.","suggestedLevel":"proficient","excerpts":[{"artifact":"work","quote":"Ice melts faster on a metal plate than on a plastic plate."}],"uncertainty":"low"},{"criterionId":"evidence","observation":"You used data from all three trials, reported the times with units, and compared the two plates for each trial. Your statement that metal was faster by at least 6 minutes in every trial is supported by your numbers. To make the comparison even stronger, you could calculate an average melting time for each plate.","suggestedLevel":"proficient","excerpts":[{"artifact":"work","quote":"In trial 1 the metal ice melted in 4 minutes and the plastic ice melted in 10 minutes."},{"artifact":"work","quote":"Every single trial metal was faster by at least 6 minutes."}],"uncertainty":"low"},{"criterionId":"reasoning","observation":"You correctly identified that metal is a conductor and plastic is not, which is a good starting point. To fully explain your results, describe the energy transfer: ice melts when it absorbs thermal energy, and a conductor transfers thermal energy from the warmer plate and room into the ice more quickly. Phrases like 'good at heat' are vague, so try 'transfers thermal energy quickly' and explain that faster energy transfer means the ice melts sooner. Then connect this back to why plastic slowed the melting.","suggestedLevel":"developing","excerpts":[{"artifact":"work","quote":"Conductors are good at heat."},{"artifact":"work","quote":"Plastic is not a conductor so it is not good at heat."}],"uncertainty":"medium"}]}
```

```json
{"findings":[{"criterionId":"claim","observation":"Your claim answers the question directly: metal plates melt ice faster. Stating it clearly at the start helps the reader know what you are arguing. You might drop 'I think' and 'at least it did for us' so the claim sounds more confident, since your data is the support for it.","suggestedLevel":"proficient","excerpts":[{"artifact":"work","quote":"I think metal plates make ice melt quicker, at least it did for us."}],"uncertainty":"low"},{"criterionId":"evidence","observation":"You point to the metal plate's melting time, but the evidence needs more precision. 'Around 4 minutes' is approximate, and 'a lot longer' gives no number for the plastic plate. Include the actual times from your three trials for each plate, and state whether the metal times were consistently shorter than the plastic times. Specific numbers from your own data will make the claim much stronger.","suggestedLevel":"developing","excerpts":[{"artifact":"work","quote":"The metal ones melted around 4 minutes and plastic ones were a lot longer."}],"uncertainty":"medium"},{"criterionId":"reasoning","observation":"You use a good set of science ideas: energy moves from warmer to colder objects, metal is a conductor, and plastic is an insulator, which explains why the ice gets energy at different rates. Your idea that the metal keeps replacing the energy the ice loses is a clear way to connect the mechanism to the faster melting. To make the reasoning even stronger, explain why the ice needs energy to melt and how the rate of energy transfer relates to the time it took to melt.","suggestedLevel":"proficient","excerpts":[{"artifact":"work","quote":"Metal is a good conductor so energy from the whole plate can travel to the ice fast and keep replacing the energy the ice took."},{"artifact":"work","quote":"Plastic is an insulator and slows that down, so the ice gets less energy per minute."}],"uncertainty":"medium"}]}
```

```json
{"findings":[{"criterionId":"claim","observation":"You show that the plates made a difference, and your last sentence implies metal is the faster one. Try stating the answer directly in one sentence, such as \"Metal melts ice faster than plastic.\" A clear claim at the start helps the reader know what you are arguing.","suggestedLevel":"developing","excerpts":[{"artifact":"work","quote":"The plates ain't melt the ice the same."}],"uncertainty":"medium"},{"criterionId":"evidence","observation":"Your evidence is strong. You reported all three trials for each plate with times, and the metal times (3:30, 4:00, 4:25) are clearly shorter than the plastic times (10:20, 11:50, 14:00). Your comparison of about three times as long is a useful way to show the size of the difference.","suggestedLevel":"proficient","excerpts":[{"artifact":"work","quote":"On metal it took 3:30, 4:00 and 4:25."},{"artifact":"work","quote":"On plastic it took 10:20, 11:50 and 14:00."},{"artifact":"work","quote":"That's why it took like three times as long on the plastic."}],"uncertainty":"low"},{"criterionId":"reasoning","observation":"You are right that the material matters, but the idea of \"cold\" being given to the ice is a common misconception. Cold is not a substance that moves from one object to another. Both plates started at room temperature, so the plates are warmer than the ice, and thermal energy flows from the warmer plate into the ice. Metal feels colder when you touch it because it conducts thermal energy away from your hand quickly. That same fast conduction lets metal deliver thermal energy to the ice faster than plastic does. Try rewriting your explanation using thermal energy, heat flow, and conduction, and explain why the metal transfers energy faster.","suggestedLevel":"beginning","excerpts":[{"artifact":"work","quote":"Metal be cold when you touch it, colder than the plastic"},{"artifact":"work","quote":"so the metal giving its cold to the ice and the ice don't need it"},{"artifact":"work","quote":"Plastic ain't got no cold in it like that"}],"uncertainty":"medium"}]}
```

